I'm not an expert, but as far as I know vowel length is not important  
in Lojban. You can pronounce them as long or short as you like. The  
only case where it's reccommended to use shorter vowels if if you're  
adding pronounced vowels that aren't written. For example, if {mlatu}  
is hard to say, you can stick a very short vowel -- any one that's  
not already used, such as the {i} in {bit} in American English -- in  
between {m} and {l}.

But that's a special case, and I'd say you should probably learn to  
speak more mainstream Lojban without the buffer vowels. The normal  
vowels don't have any particular length requirements.

